```c
int i;
```
```c
while( vlc_object_alive (p_demux) && i < 40 && Demux2( p_demux, false ) > 0 ) i++;
```
```c
while( vlc_object_alive (p_demux) && i < 40 && Demux2( p_demux, true ) > 0 );
```
Reason:
The variable `i` was declared outside of the loop, which means its value persists across multiple iterations and function calls. This can lead to incorrect loop conditions and increments, potentially causing infinite loops or incorrect processing of the demuxing operation, as the value of `i` may not reset as expected.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
int i = 0;
```
```c
for( int i = 0; i < PS_TK_COUNT; i++ )
```
Reason:
By declaring `i` within the scope of the loop (using `int i = 0;`), it ensures that `i` is reset to 0 each time the loop is entered. This prevents the logical errors associated with the previous declaration, allowing the loops to terminate correctly and process the demuxing operation as intended.


